      "id": "nc150-task-scheduler",
      "front": "**Task Scheduler** · Medium\\n\\n[LeetCode](https://leetcode.com/problems/task-scheduler/)\\n\\nGiven task letters and a cooldown n between identical tasks, find the minimum number of time units (idles allowed) to finish all.\\n\\nPattern? Approach?",
      "back": "**Pattern:** Greedy counting (or max-heap + cooldown queue)\\n\\n**Key insight:** The most frequent task forms (maxCount-1) frames of length n+1, plus one slot per task tied for max; the answer is at least the task count.\\n\\n**Java**\\n\\n\`\`\`java\\nclass Solution {\\n    public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {\\n        int[] cnt = new int[26];\\n        for (char t : tasks) cnt[t - 'A']++;\\n        int max = 0, tied = 0;\\n        for (int c : cnt) {\\n            if (c > max) { max = c; tied = 1; }\\n            else if (c == max) tied++;\\n        }\\n        return Math.max(tasks.length, (max - 1) * (n + 1) + tied);\\n    }\\n}\\n\`\`\`\\n\\n**Python**\\n\\n\`\`\`python\\nfrom collections import Counter\\n\\nclass Solution:\\n    def leastInterval(self, tasks: List[str], n: int) -> int:\\n        cnt = Counter(tasks)\\n        mx = max(cnt.values())\\n        tied = sum(1 for c in cnt.values() if c == mx)\\n        return max(len(tasks), (mx - 1) * (n + 1) + tied)\\n\`\`\`\\n\\n**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)\\n\\n**Pitfall:** Forgetting the \`max(tasks.length, ...)\` — with many distinct tasks there are no idles.",
