      "id": "nc150-partition-equal-subset-sum",
      "front": "**Partition Equal Subset Sum** · Medium\\n\\n[LeetCode](https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-equal-subset-sum/)\\n\\nDecide whether the array splits into two subsets of equal sum.\\n\\nPattern? Approach?",
      "back": "**Pattern:** 0/1 knapsack (subset sum)\\n\\n**Key insight:** Target is sum/2; iterate sums downward so each number is used at most once.\\n\\n**Java**\\n\\n\`\`\`java\\nclass Solution {\\n    public boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {\\n        int sum = 0;\\n        for (int x : nums) sum += x;\\n        if (sum % 2 == 1) return false;\\n        int t = sum / 2;\\n        boolean[] can = new boolean[t + 1];\\n        can[0] = true;\\n        for (int x : nums)\\n            for (int s = t; s >= x; s--) can[s] |= can[s - x];\\n        return can[t];\\n    }\\n}\\n\`\`\`\\n\\n**Python**\\n\\n\`\`\`python\\nfrom typing import List\\n\\nclass Solution:\\n    def canPartition(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:\\n        total = sum(nums)\\n        if total % 2:\\n            return False\\n        t = total // 2\\n        can = [True] + [False] * t\\n        for x in nums:\\n            for s in range(t, x - 1, -1):\\n                can[s] = can[s] or can[s - x]\\n        return can[t]\\n\`\`\`\\n\\n**Time/Space:** O(n · sum) / O(sum)\\n\\n**Pitfall:** Looping sums upward reuses the same item (turns it into unbounded knapsack).",
